<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Lament · c. 1250 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Dies irae</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A medieval Latin chant about the end of the world, sung at funerals; its gloomy tune turns up in many film scores.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>attributed to Thomas of Celano or Latino Malabranca Orsini</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous (13th-century Gregorian chant)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Latin Christians of medieval Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>13th century at the latest (manuscript of 1253–1255)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words attributed to Thomas of Celano (d. 1265) or Latino Malabranca Orsini (d. 1294))</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dies-irae">Dies irae</a>, &quot;the Day of Wrath&quot;, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> sequence, a hymn of the Mass. It is attributed either to the Franciscan Thomas of Celano (1200–1265) or to Latino Malabranca Orsini (d. 1294), lector at the Dominican house of studies at Santa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>; some sources take it back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Gregory the Great</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a> or Bonaventure. It dates from the 13th century at the latest. Its earliest known text is in a Franciscan calendar missal now in the national library at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, which must date from 1253–1255 because it lacks the name of Clare of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a>, canonised in 1255. It is a rhymed poem in accented trochaic metre. Its images come above all from the Vulgate text of the prophet Zephaniah, with others from Revelation, Matthew, Thessalonians, Peter and Luke; the Jewish prayer Unetanneh Tokef appears to be related.</p><p>For centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dies-irae">Dies irae</a> was the sequence of the Requiem Mass in the Roman Rite and part of the liturgy of All Souls' Day. Its last two stanzas differ in form from the rest, and some scholars think they were added to fit the poem for liturgical use. In the reforms that followed the Second Vatican Council, the Consilium charged with them (1969–70) removed the sequence from funerals and other Masses for the Dead; Archbishop Annibale Bugnini explained that texts dwelling on judgment, fear and despair were replaced with ones expressing Christian hope. It is still sung where the Tridentine liturgy of the 1962 Missal is used, and, slightly edited and divided into three parts, remains an optional hymn of the Liturgy of the Hours on All Souls' Day and in the last week before Advent. In the edit a line naming Mary was changed, because modern scholarship no longer identifies Mary Magdalene with the woman taken in adultery.</p><p>The 13th-century Gregorian melody is a sombre chant in the Dorian mode and one of the most quoted tunes in music. The earliest surviving polyphonic Requiem, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-ockeghem">Johannes Ockeghem</a>, does not include it; the first settings to include it are by Engarandus Juvenis (1490) and Antoine Brumel (1516), and later ones by Charpentier, Delalande, Mozart, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hector-berlioz">Berlioz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Verdi</a>, Britten and Stravinsky. Berlioz's use of the chant in the Symphonie fantastique made it widely known and tied it, especially in the 19th century, to death and terror. Liszt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camille-saint-saens">Saint-Saëns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-mahler">Mahler</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modest-mussorgsky">Mussorgsky</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Tchaikovsky</a> and Rachmaninoff, in more than a dozen works, quoted it, and it has been used in many film scores and popular works, from the score for Metropolis (1927) and Citizen Kane (1941) to The Shining (1980) and The Lion King.</p><p>The poem itself travelled through translation and literature. William <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a> Irons made an English version in 1849 that keeps the rhyme and metre; edited, it is approved for funerals in the Catholic ordinariates for former Anglicans. The Irish priest Bernard Callan put it into Irish around 1800, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-newton">John Newton</a> made a loose Protestant version, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a> Bierce a satirical one in 1903. Walter Scott used two stanzas in The Lay of the Last Minstrel (1805), Goethe three in the cathedral scene of Faust (1808), and Oscar Wilde wrote a sonnet on hearing it sung in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a> (1881).</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A meditation on the Last Judgment. It pictures the end of the world, a trumpet calling the dead before the throne of God and a book from which all are judged, and the singer, afraid of being among the condemned, pleads for mercy. Its closing stanzas turn to a prayer for rest for the dead.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Latin</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">DIES IRÆ, DIES ILLA!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>DAY of Wrath! O Day of mourning!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Solvet sæclum in favillâ,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>See! once more the Cross returning,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Teste David cum Sybillâ.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Heav'n and earth in ashes burning!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Quantus tremor est futurus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O what fear man's bosom rendeth,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Quando Judex est venturus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When from Heav'n the Judge descendeth,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Cuncta stricte discussurus.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On whose sentence all dependeth!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Tuba mirum spargens sonum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Wondrous sound the Trumpet flingeth,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Per sepulcra regionum,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Through earth's sepulchres it ringeth,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Coget omnes ante thronum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All before the throne it bringeth!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Mors stupebit, et natura,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Death is struck, and nature quaking,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Quum resurget creatura,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All creation is awaking,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Judicanti responsura.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To its Judge an answer making!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Liber scriptus proferetur,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lo, the Book, exactly worded!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">In quo totum continetur,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Wherein all hath been recorded;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Unde mundus judicetur.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thence shall judgment be awarded.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Judex ergo cum sedebit,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When the Judge His seat attaineth,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Quidquid latet, apparebit:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And each hidden deed arraigneth,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Nil inultum remanebit.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Nothing unaveng'd remaineth.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 55 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Quid sum, miser! tunc dicturus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What shall I, frail man, be pleading,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Quem patronum rogaturus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who for me be interceding,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Quum vix justus sit securus?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When the just are mercy needing?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Rex tremendæ majestatis,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>King of majesty tremendous,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Qui salvandos salvas gratis,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who dost free salvation send us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Salva me, fons pietatis!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Fount of pity! then befriend us!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Recordare, Jesu pie,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Think! Kind Jesu, my salvation</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Quod sum causa tuæ viæ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Caus'd Thy wondrous Incarnation;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ne me perdas illâ die!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Leave me not to reprobation!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Quærens me, sedisti lassus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Faint and weary Thou hast sought me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Redemisti, crucem passus:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On the Cross of suffering bought me;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Tantus labor non sit cassus.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shall such grace be vainly brought me!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Juste Judex ultionis,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Righteous Judge of retribution,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Donum fac remissionis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Grant Thy gift of absolution,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ante diem rationis.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ere that reck'ning day's conclusion!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ingemisco tanquam reus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Guilty, now I pour my moaning,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Culpâ rubet vultus meus;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All my shame with anguish owning;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Supplicanti parce, Deus!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Spare, O God, Thy suppliant, groaning!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Qui Mariam absolvisti,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thou, the sinful woman savest,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Et latronem exaudisti,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thou, the dying thief forgavest;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Mihi quoque spem dedisti.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And to me a hope vouchsafest!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Preces meæ non sunt dignæ,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Worthless are my pray'rs and sighing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Sed Tu bonus fac benigne</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Yet, good Lord, in grace complying,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ne perenni cremer igne!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rescue me from fires undying!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Inter oves locum præsta,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With Thy favor'd sheep, O place me!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Et ab hædis me sequestra,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Nor among the goats abase me;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Statuens in parte dextrâ.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But to Thy right hand upraise me.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Confutatis maledictis,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>While the wicked are confounded,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Flammis acribus addictis,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Doom'd to flames of woe unbounded,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Voca me cum benedictis!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Call me! with Thy saints surrounded.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Oro supplex et acclinis,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Low I kneel, with heart submission;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Cor contritum quasi cinis,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>See, like ashes, my contrition;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Gere curam mei finis.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Help me, in my last condition!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Lacrymosa dies illa!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! that Day of tears and mourning!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Qua resurget ex favillâ.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From the dust of earth returning,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Judicandus homo reus;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Man for judgment must prepare him;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Huic ergo parce, Deus!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Spare! O God, in mercy, spare him!</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Attributed to Thomas of Celano (13th century); as printed in The Seven Great Hymns of the Mediaeval Church (1866). Text from <a href="https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/The_seven_great_hymns_of_the_mediaeval_church%2FDies_Ir%C3%A6%2FTommaso" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: Translated by William Josiah Irons (1849), as printed in The Seven Great Hymns of the Mediaeval Church (1866). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_seven_great_hymns_of_the_mediaeval_church%2FDies_Ir%C3%A6%2FIrons" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
